The current version indexes a 64 KB table directly by message number. This rewrite shrinks it to a 1024-entry direct table for the low message numbers that nearly all files use. That makes the table about 1 KB (2 KB if `MESGS >= 255`), so the hot lookups stay in L1 cache. I have not timed this change.

- **Low numbers (below 1024):** they get the same answer as before. The table is built in reverse order, so it still returns the first matching entry, exactly as the full 64 KB table did.
- **Numbers 1024 and above:** these (e.g. the manufacturer-specific 0xFF00+ range) fall back to a linear search that also returns the first match, so results are identical.
- **Possible slowdown:** if the test corpus contains many manufacturer-specific messages, each one now pays for a linear scan. Unknown numbers in that range scan all entries. That case could be slower than the current version.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    typedef typename std::conditional<((int)MESGS < 255), FIT_UINT8, FIT_UINT16>::type IdxT;
    enum { SMALL = 1024 };
    struct Index
    {
        IdxT idx[SMALL];
        Index()
        {
            const IdxT none = (IdxT)~(IdxT)0;
            for (int k = 0; k < SMALL; k++)
                idx[k] = none;
            for (int i = MESGS - 1; i >= 0; i--)
            {
                const FIT_UINT16 n = (FIT_UINT16)mesgs[i].num;
                if (n < SMALL)
                    idx[n] = (IdxT)i;
            }
        }
    };
    static const Index table;

    if (num < SMALL)
    {
        const IdxT i = table.idx[num];
        if (i == (IdxT)~(IdxT)0)
            return NULL;
        return &mesgs[i];
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < (int)MESGS; i++)
    {
        if ((FIT_UINT16)mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }
    return NULL;
}
```